## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Our Garden.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Tall Tree!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Use gestures and demonstrations to help classmates perform a simple task. Concept: Use a word and a gesture or drawing to describe a familiar object.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures or body language to describe plants and trees to classmates (e.g., miming being a tree by stretching the arms upward or pretending to hold a bouquet of flowers). Can clarify responses about nature by pointing to garden objects or drawing pictures (e.g., drawing a simple flower or a tree on the board). Can use simple gestures or drawings to explain feelings about the garden (e.g., pointing to a tall tree in response to the question, "What do you like most in the garden?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and leads a quick review of garden vocabulary using flashcards and Total Physical Response (TPR). Students mime actions like "grow" (stretch arms up), "water" (sprinkle with hands), and describe items like "tall tree" (stretch arms high) or "pink flower" (touch cheek and make a flower shape with hands). Teacher says, "Show me a tall tree!" or "Show me how a flower grows!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models how to *mediate* information using gestures and simple drawings. For instance, the teacher holds up a flashcard of a "tall tree" and then uses gestures (stretching arms upward, pointing to the top) to emphasize "tall." The teacher then draws a simple flower on the board to illustrate "flower" and asks, "What color is this flower?" The teacher responds with a color and points to the drawing. The teacher emphasizes, "We use our hands and drawings to help our friends understand!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of mediation cues.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher mimes a "short plant" and asks, "Is this a <b>tall tree</b>?" (Expected answer: "No!" or shaking head).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher draws a simple sun and points to it, then asks, "Is this a <b>flower</b>?" (Expected answer: "No!" or pointing to a flower flashcard).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Our Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A "Big Book" or a sequence of large, laminated picture cards showing children in a garden. The story depicts a child trying to find a specific item (e.g., "a pink flower") and then another child using gestures and pointing to help them locate it. The pictures should clearly illustrate the action of finding, describing, and mediating.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Talking about nature: Describe plants and trees with singular and plural phrases (e.g., "The tree is tall." "The flowers are pink."). Asking questions in nature: Use basic questions like "What color is the flower?" to learn about plants.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher presents the picture story, narrating with simple sentences like, "This is a garden. Oh, look! She wants a pink flower. He helps her! He points to the pink flower."
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are the children doing?" (Expected: "Garden!" or "Find!"). "Are they happy?" (Expected: "Yes!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Point and identify:</b> Teacher points to a picture and asks, "What is this?" (Expected: "Tree!" or "Flower!"). "Is this a big tree or a small tree?" (Expected: "Big!").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using gestures and simple drawings to mediate understanding of garden vocabulary. Grammar: Noun + verb + adjective (e.g., "The flowers are pink."), Present simple (e.g., "This is a tall tree."). Vocabulary: garden, tree, flower, plant, leaf, bush, sun, pink, green, tall, short, small, big. Pronunciation: /g/ in "garden," /t/ in "tree," /s/ in "sun."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Gesture & Word Match:</b> The teacher calls out a garden word (e.g., "tall tree," "pink flower," "green leaf"). Students respond by making the corresponding gesture or drawing a quick, simple representation on individual mini-whiteboards. Conversely, the teacher makes a gesture or drawing, and students say the word aloud chorally or individually. The teacher provides immediate feedback on accuracy of gestures/drawings and pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled: Draw-to-Explain Relay:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4). The first student in each group receives a flashcard with a garden item (e.g., "a small bush"). They draw the item on a piece of paper or mini-whiteboard without speaking and pass it to the next student. The second student, after seeing the drawing, has to make a gesture to explain it to the third student (e.g., miming a "small bush"). The third student then points to a flashcard from a pile of mixed garden items. The teacher circulates, observing and providing support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Help Me Find It!" Game:</b> Students work in pairs with a set of garden flashcards spread out face up. Student A says, "I see a ____." (e.g., "I see a tall tree."). Student B must point to the correct flashcard. If Student B is confused, Student A must use a gesture (e.g., stretching arms up for "tall tree") or a quick drawing on paper to help Student B understand and find the correct card. Students swap roles.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Our Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Mini Garden Scavenger Hunt Explanation (21st Century Skills Project - Mediation Focus)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives an "objective card" with a simple instruction and a picture of what to find in the school garden (e.g., "Find: a tall tree," "Find: a pink flower," "Find: a green leaf"). Due to the focus on mediation, one student in the group acts as the "explainer" (mediator). The explainer must convey the instruction to their classmates *only* using gestures and simple drawings on a small whiteboard or paper provided (no speaking). The other students in the group interpret these mediated instructions and then identify the object from a collection of large garden flashcards placed around the classroom (simulating the school garden). Once identified, the group collaboratively "labels" it by drawing a simple picture and saying the sentence, "This is a tall tree," or "The flower is pink." The teacher circulates to provide support and observe the mediation process.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> In pairs, students receive a new picture of a garden item (e.g., "a small green bush"). Student A uses gestures or draws the item to explain it to Student B without speaking. Student B then points to the correct item on a large classroom garden poster or from a set of mixed garden flashcards. The teacher observes pairs for effective mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible gestures/drawings: Can the partner understand the visual/non-verbal cues?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (conveyed non-verbally): Are "tall," "pink," "flower," "tree" effectively communicated?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining the item: Is the item easily identifiable from the mediation?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration: Do students work together to achieve understanding?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick drawing activity.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I show my friend a tall tree using my hands?" (Students draw a happy face or a sad face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I draw a pink flower for my friend to understand?" (Students draw a happy face or a sad face).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you do today to help your friend understand about the garden?" (e.g., "I used my hands," "I drew a picture").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or hard to explain with gestures or drawings?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? Did students effectively use gestures and simple drawings to explain garden objects and instructions for the scavenger hunt?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with non-verbal communication? Perhaps more direct modeling of specific gestures or simpler drawing templates.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did the 21st Century Skills Project (Garden Scavenger Hunt) enhance the mediation focus for 1st graders?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> (This is the final lesson in the sequence.) This lesson serves as a capstone, consolidating communicative skills through mediation. The next sequence will introduce a new theme and scenario, building on the foundation of collaborative communication and basic vocabulary acquisition.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>